These are release notes for NEMO-3D.

WHAT IS NEMO-3D?
================

In brief, NEMO-3D is a tight-binding based electronic structure solver.  
At the moment, only zinc-blende structures have been implemented, but 
the code has been written in a sufficiently general way that it can be 
readily modified to handle any kind of atomic connectivity.  The electronic 
structure component also includes postprocessing tools (described in 
NEMO3D/postprocessing/README) to compute electron-hole exchange energies 
and dipole moments.  This release of NEMO-3D is still considered 
preliminary and is *not* currently ready for mass consumption.  There is,
for example, no simple GUI to guide the simulation, and the code is not
rebust and fault-tolerant enough for casual use.  However, it is under
active development and we feel it is nevertheless in a state where it may
be useful to those interested in a tight-binding-based electronic structure
solver.  For details regarding the physics of the NEMO-3D please refer 
first to the list of publications below.  More publications and additional 
documentation is available at http://hpc.jpl.nasa.gov/PEP/gekco/journal.html.


DOCUMENTATION
=============

installation:
After untarring NEMO3D.tar.gz, run the "nemo3d_setup.sh" script to
setup the third party packages (Xerces C++, LAPACK, BLAS, ARPACK, PARPACK),
symbolic links, and patches.  Please cd into the build directory and read 
the README and BUILD for build instructions.

execution:
Please read nemo3d/example/README for (brief) information on running NEMO3D.


